> Detailed process impact IAMs
> In contrast to the relatively simple and stylized cost–benefit IAMs that
> have been used to calculate the SCC, there is a class of IAMs with
> higher spatial and process resolution that couple biogeophysical and
> economic models to represent climate impacts at finer scales, referred
> to as detailed process IAMs by Weyant14. Examples of these types of
> IAMs include the Global Change Assessment Model (GCAM)15, the
> Integrated Global Systems Model (IGSM)16 and the Integrated Model
> to Assess the Global Environment (IMAGE)17, among others.
> These models play an integral role in global scenario assess-
> ments (for example the RCPs18 and shared socioeconomic pathways
> (SSPs)19), but have not typically been used to estimate economic risks
> directly because they report outcomes in terms of resources or other
> physical measures, as opposed to monetized impacts, consumption
> or welfare. However, using them to inform the calibration of IAM
