> Can you propose some multiple choice questions to check if they've done the reading?
**assistant**:
> ### Reading-check multiple-choice questions
> 
> 1. **What does the social cost of carbon (SCC) estimate?**  
>    A. The cost of extracting a tonne of coal  
>    B. The climate damages caused over time by one additional tonne of CO₂  
>    C. The cost of meeting a national emissions target  
>    D. The market price of carbon offsets  
>    **Answer: B**
> 
> 2. **In cost–benefit integrated assessment models, what is a damage function?**  
>    A. A climate-model parameter governing warming  
>    B. A relationship translating climate variables into economic welfare losses  
>    C. A forecast of annual emissions  
>    D. A measure of adaptation spending alone  
>    **Answer: B**
> 
> 3. **Which three IAMs were used in the US government’s SCC estimates discussed in the article?**  
>    A. DICE, FUND, and PAGE  
>    B. GCAM, IMAGE, and REMIND  
>    C. MAGICC, WITCH, and RICE  
>    D. PAGE, IMAGE, and MAGICC  
>    **Answer: A**
> 
> 4. **Which model is described as projecting net global benefits at relatively low levels of warming?**  
>    A. DICE  
>    B. FUND  
>    C. PAGE  
>    D. None of them  
>    **Answer: B**
> 
> 5. **What is “dynamic vulnerability” in the context of FUND?**  
>    A. The possibility of abrupt climate tipping points  
>    B. The assumption that vulnerability changes with income growth and technological change  
>    C. The effect of year-to-year weather variability  
>    D. The cost of emergency disaster response  
>    **Answer: B**
> 
> 6. **Why are existing damage functions especially uncertain at high temperatures?**  
>    A. They use no climate data  
>    B. They are generally calibrated at roughly 1–3 °C warming but extrapolated beyond that range  
>    C. High temperatures affect only non-market sectors  
>    D. Models cease to include adaptation above 3 °C  
>    **Answer: B**
> 
> 7. **Which is *not* identified as a common limitation of conventional IAM damage functions?**  
>    A. Incomplete coverage of impact categories  
>    B. Neglect of interactions across sectors and regions  
>    C. Excessively detailed representation of local institutions  
>    D. Inadequate treatment of thresholds and irreversibility  
>    **Answer: C**
> 
> 8. **What is a key advantage of empirical climate-impact studies?**  
>    A. They directly observe statistical relationships between climate/weather and real-world outcomes  
>    B. They eliminate the need to value non-market impacts  
>    C. They always provide global coverage  
>    D. They model every causal mechanism explicitly  
>    **Answer: A**
